questions.question:
The following infinite-scroll code lives inside a custom scrollable panel (a `<div id="panel" style="overflow-y:auto; height:400px">`), with a sentinel element as the last child:

```js
const sentinel = document.querySelector('#sentinel');

const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  entries.forEach(entry => {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
      loadMoreItems();
    }
  });
});

observer.observe(sentinel);
```

In production, `loadMoreItems()` either never fires as users scroll the panel, or fires many times in rapid succession for a single scroll-into-view event. Identify both bugs and fix the code.

questions.correctAnswer:
Bug 1 (never fires / fires incorrectly): the observer is created without a `root` option, so it defaults to the browser viewport, not the `#panel` scroll container. Intersection is being computed against the wrong scrolling ancestor, so entries can report intersecting/non-intersecting states that don't match what the user actually sees inside the panel. Fix: pass `{ root: document.querySelector('#panel') }` (and optionally a `rootMargin` like '200px' to prefetch before the sentinel is fully visible).

Bug 2 (fires many times rapidly): `loadMoreItems()` is called on every intersection callback invocation while `isIntersecting` stays true (e.g., before new content pushes the sentinel out of view, or due to multiple threshold crossings), causing duplicate/overlapping fetches. Fix: guard with a loading flag (or unobserve/reobserve the sentinel) so a new load can't start while one is in flight, e.g.:

```js
let isLoading = false;
const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  entries.forEach(async entry => {
    if (entry.isIntersecting && !isLoading) {
      isLoading = true;
      await loadMoreItems();
      isLoading = false;
    }
  });
}, { root: document.querySelector('#panel'), rootMargin: '200px' });

observer.observe(sentinel);
```

questions.question:
This custom React hook is meant to run a per-frame animation loop:

```js
function useAnimation(callback) {
  useEffect(() => {
    function loop() {
      callback();
      requestAnimationFrame(loop);
    }
    requestAnimationFrame(loop);
  }, [callback]);
}
```

In practice, components using this hook see animation speed increase over time, occasional errors referencing unmounted DOM nodes, and higher-than-expected CPU usage the longer a page stays open. Identify all bugs and rewrite the hook correctly.

questions.correctAnswer:
Bug 1 — no cleanup / no cancellation: the effect never calls `cancelAnimationFrame`, so on unmount (or whenever the effect re-runs) the existing `loop` keeps scheduling itself forever, continuing to run and touch state/DOM after the component is gone (causing the 'unmounted node' errors and a memory/CPU leak that never stops).

Bug 2 — unstable dependency causes duplicate loops: the effect depends on `callback`. If the parent doesn't memoize `callback` (no `useCallback`), a new function identity is created every render, re-running the effect and starting a *new* `requestAnimationFrame(loop)` chain each time without ever canceling the previous one — so multiple loops stack up and run concurrently, which explains animation speed increasing over time (multiple loops each calling `callback()` per frame).

Fix — cancel on cleanup, and decouple the loop's identity from `callback` via a ref so re-renders don't spawn new loops:

```js
function useAnimation(callback) {
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);
  useEffect(() => { callbackRef.current = callback; }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    let rafId;
    function loop() {
      callbackRef.current();
      rafId = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
    }
    rafId = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);
  }, []); // stable — starts exactly one loop, cleaned up on unmount
}
```

questions.question:
A widget syncs a visual counter by watching its own `data-count` attribute and mirroring it into a CSS custom property, but also increments the attribute in the same callback to 'debounce' external updates:

```js
const el = document.getElementById('widget');

const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
  mutations.forEach(m => {
    if (m.type === 'attributes' && m.attributeName === 'data-count') {
      const count = parseInt(el.dataset.count, 10);
      el.dataset.count = count + 1;
      el.style.setProperty('--count', count);
    }
  });
});

observer.observe(el, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['data-count'] });
```

As soon as any code sets `el.dataset.count = 'someValue'` once, the tab freezes. Explain exactly why, and rewrite the code to fix it.

questions.correctAnswer:
The callback mutates the very attribute (`data-count`) it is observing. Each time the callback runs, `el.dataset.count = count + 1` itself is a mutation on the observed attribute, which generates a new MutationRecord that gets queued for the same observer. Because MutationObserver delivery keeps draining its queue until empty within the microtask checkpoint, this creates unbounded self-triggering recursion — the callback keeps re-invoking itself synchronously as fast as the engine can process microtasks, with no exit condition, hanging the tab.

Fix: break the self-observation loop, either by disconnecting before mutating and reconnecting after, or — better — by not observing an attribute you also write to from the same callback (separate the 'observed' state from the 'derived' state):

```js
const el = document.getElementById('widget');

const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
  mutations.forEach(m => {
    if (m.type === 'attributes' && m.attributeName === 'data-count') {
      observer.disconnect(); // stop observing before we mutate
      const count = parseInt(el.dataset.count, 10);
      el.style.setProperty('--count', count);
      // if incrementing is truly needed, do it here, then:
      observer.observe(el, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['data-count'] });
    }
  });
});

observer.observe(el, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['data-count'] });
```

A cleaner design avoids the increment-inside-callback pattern entirely: use a separate attribute/property for the 'source of truth' the observer watches, and write derived output (like the CSS variable) to a different attribute so the callback never mutates its own trigger.
